        <ArticleTitle>Parallel initiation of four-pillar therapy in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.</ArticleTitle>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Sequential titration of disease-modifying therapy in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction delays achievement of target doses by a median of 6 months.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We randomised 1204 adults with a left ventricular ejection fraction of 40 percent or below to parallel low-dose initiation of all four agent classes or to standard sequential titration.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">At 12 weeks, 71 percent of the parallel group were receiving all four classes compared with 34 percent of the sequential group. Rates of symptomatic hypotension were <i>not</i> significantly different.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Parallel initiation achieved guideline-directed medical therapy substantially faster without an excess of treatment-limiting adverse events.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
